%N A400977 Start with a set of four adjacent collinear points in a hexagonal lattice. Every iteration, add to the set the lattice points which are adjacent to exactly two points already in the set, only if those two points are adjacent to each other. This sequence gives the number of points in the set after n iterations.
%C A400977 Pattern exhibits periodic bird-shaped clusters of holes and polymer-like chains like those discussed in the comments of A400002 (see illustration for n = 100).
%C A400977 Starting with two points yields A400002, and starting with an odd number k of adjacent collinear points produces a sequence that terminates after k-1 iterations. For example, starting with five such points yields 5,13,23,27,33,33,33,... . If we start with any number k of adjacent collinear points, then for n = 0,1,...,k-1, the number of points after n iterations is k + n*(2*k-n+1) - 2*(n mod 2).
